#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076ccae2-d102-5ad8-b1c2-ac28978e3e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263068} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3882de-2108-52bf-b5e1-4c3304b9a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263257} {:answer/id #uuid "a00ce4f7-3888-5dba-a972-b715615f1421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2a0770-0459-500c-aa32-352ae1667e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264813} {:answer/id #uuid "d493ac59-d9ae-5896-bd9e-006d111d56b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00ce4f7-3888-5dba-a972-b715615f1421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7e101e-fe81-5db4-a578-c892744685d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c80428ab-daca-506e-aca7-d4e40b0d604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130718" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4345e16b-0df8-59b8-ad37-650db4ac210e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,3,-5⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263593} {:answer/id #uuid "58520017-6d75-5711-9c4c-279ff100c302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨-2,5,-3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263761} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8b5e1d-96ce-502e-ac9c-14b8cdee3daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264220} {:answer/id #uuid "a89fe459-90af-559f-b732-4d06764bcb9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264441} {:answer/id #uuid "e315f8f8-9238-56c6-b08f-3a589b1dff97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨4,5,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a89fe459-90af-559f-b732-4d06764bcb9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264441} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90461b1-1149-54d3-9d43-9942adac1495" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b9d3e0c-64e0-51a4-97be-e5302f43c4c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131126" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x=5$ at the point $(1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=5.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)k \\\\  & =(2xy+{z}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+2zx)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(2(1)(-1)+(2)^{2})\\text{ }i+((1)^{2}+2(-1)(2))\\text{ }j+((-1)^{2}+2(2)(1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,∇f(1,-1,2)=⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2f7fab-cebe-519a-83e3-a0831cac289d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Plane Curve Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "034d9a67-8763-5c70-b5b9-7febdebfdaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-5y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496664} {:answer/id #uuid "132dcf6f-8423-544a-b9b0-4fe0220a1869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-3y+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496700} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc0248f-f177-570e-9398-26b743d6c5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x-2y+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496894} {:answer/id #uuid "9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+5y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496982} {:answer/id #uuid "bc272b8f-4ab5-5f01-8506-f12b4f57de0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x-4y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+5y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496982} :answer-field/id #uuid "22b9919e-c058-55c4-a73e-14ca053dbdce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb219d72-040d-55dd-90ca-3f831acb4d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-65173" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(2(2)-(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨5,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{5} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-4} \\\\ -4(x-2) & =5(y+1) \\\\ -4x-5y+8-5 & =0 \\\\ -4x-5y+3 & =0 \\\\ 4x+5y-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4643060a-8d1d-5789-a362-102d70382db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398956} {:answer/id #uuid "8070bb66-d16c-505c-85ee-2a417bc81d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398957} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdcaf70-4358-5973-a594-026557c83288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398958} {:answer/id #uuid "61e68fe8-0332-53d0-b214-d38a39b52899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398959} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c9c882-083f-5978-8947-be71bba2f10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4643060a-8d1d-5789-a362-102d70382db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398956} :answer-field/id #uuid "91095769-6695-5735-bdf7-d8d4bfdca38b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58a67c4a-aa8b-5b64-8268-56928e2716de" :question/math-academy-id "q-71399" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,0) & =({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,i+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,j+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Normal Line to a Surface Using the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "367ab4f2-93bc-5938-9462-dc0e018dd590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496776} {:answer/id #uuid "399e3797-afa8-52bc-9d91-1f755a50f199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496783} {:answer/id #uuid "d59ec8f6-4eba-53b8-a3b8-5a5f6ce7e72d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497104} {:answer/id #uuid "db86fc5b-7216-54fe-8379-494c5e62c9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497123} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc71725-cf86-521b-8c43-ec05f48b2500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbc71725-cf86-521b-8c43-ec05f48b2500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497200} :answer-field/id #uuid "631be222-c76c-5ee5-9b25-874887015acd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca2288f3-367d-50c0-9754-21f3665fdec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71406" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz$ at the point $(1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+yz)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xz)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(0)(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(1))\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(0))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(1,0,1)=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}]}
